Installation
------------

WATCHDOG.OPO can be copied into any \OPO directory
SYS$PSWD.OPO _must_ be in M:\OPO (the \OPO on the internal drive)

Running Watchdog will ask you for a password and to confirm the
password, this will create a dummy file M:\WATCHDOG\PASSWD.DAT with
the encrypted password in it.  You can now set the timeout and info
lines, or change the password.

Use
---

Watchdog has a full menu system, accessed via the Menu key.  Or
hotkeys are avaliable, these are the key listed after the Menu entry
without Psion pressed.  They are:

S - Set/Change password
I - Set/Change info lines (these are displayed when the password is
    requested)
T - Set/Change the time after which the password is needed, this is in
    hh:mm:ss format
N - Launch Watchdog protection
F - Switch off watchdog protection
G - Jump to watchdog for 2 seconds
U - Update the status screen
A - About display information about watchdog
R - Register enter your registration number (does not appear on
    registered version)
X - Exit watchdog (leaves watchdog protection on or off as displayed
    on Status screen)

Launching watchdog (N) will leave it resident until it is switched off
by using the (F) key, Watchdog does not need to be running for
SYS$PSWD to be active.

Setting "Time" to  00:00:00 will make watchdog act as the system
password.

Security Considerations
-----------------------

Watchdog is less secure than the system password, a soft reset or hard
reset will kill the password protection.  It should not be relied on
to protect data.  It will however deter the curious snooper from
viewing your data.  It is designed for those who find the every time
switched on password requirement sufficiently irritating they don't
use it, but would feel more comfortable with some form of password
protection from the novice user.

Currently Watchdog only traps 2 forms of "OFF" event, Psion+1 or auto
timeout.  If some other OFF is used for example "O" when using Mark D
Esposito's notepad or some other OPL or C program calling the OFF
command watchdog will not operate.

If some other process is trapping the Psion+1 key watchdog will be
unable to (an EPOC restriction - only 1 process can trap a keypress)
and will not launch.  If watchdog is running and another process tries
to trap Psion+1 the CALL will return a negative value, if the process
doesn't check for this it may exit abruptly.

